+## 6. Strongest attacks and a decomposition bypass
+
+**Strongest proof attack — `PIC-DISC`.** Resolve a hypothetical counterexample
+to a generically finite morphism `phi:X->Y` of smooth projective rational
+surfaces, of degree `d=td`. On Neron-Severi lattices, with pullback matrix
+`A`,
+
+```text
+A^T Q_X A = d Q_Y.
+```
+
+If `M=A(NS(Y))`, `Mbar` is its saturation, `h=[Mbar:M]`, and
+`K=Mbar^perp`, unimodularity gives the exact necessary identity
+
+```text
+|disc K| h^2 = d^(rho(Y)).
+```
+
+The canonical divisor formula adds
+`K_X-phi^*K_Y=R_phi>=0`, with `R_phi` supported at infinity because the
+affine Jacobian is constant nonzero. This mechanism can kill a complete
+boundary record without enumerating its local M2 coefficient descendants.
+It therefore bypasses the current decomposition after landing, although it
+does not bypass the need for a complete source and boundary morphism.
+
+**Strongest counterexample attack — K00.** Protect the already exact
+characteristic-zero seed and demand the correctly sharpened conjunction:
+nonzero/projective, source-open, rank-exact, full-`P6` compatible, then one
+prolongation and collision/nonproper witness. Do not substitute a rational
+point on the base (the origin is trivial), a rank-closed point, or one selected
+minor chart. No new heavy launch is justified by this blind round.
+
+**New connection — `COVER-MOD` (Avenues 7 + 31 + 34).** Reverse the desired
+asymptotic cover through Zariski Main and affine modifications. The first
+toric one-modification class is already an exact negative control. Put
+
+```text
+s=x,  t=(y-h(x))/x^k,
+u=s^a t^b,  v=s^c t^d.
+```
+
+Polynomiality requires `b,d>=0`, `a-kb>=0`, `c-kd>=0`, while
+
+```text
+J(u,v)=det([[a,b],[c,d]])
+       * x^(a+c-1-k(b+d)) * (y-h)^(b+d-1).
+```
+
+A nonzero constant Jacobian forces `b+d=1` and `a+c=k+1`; the polynomiality
+inequalities then force `|det|<=1`. Hence this entire one-modification toric
+class contains no degree-at-least-two cover. This is useful negative evidence,
+not permission for an unbounded two-modification search.
